    },
    "tier": "contested",
    "priority": "strong",
    "case_for": "A magnificent Blue Note sextet date pairing Hutcherson with an all-star lineup (Freddie Hubbard, James Spaulding, Herbie Hancock, Ron Carter, Joe Chambers) for a bold two-sided concept: hard bop originals on side A, avant-tinged Chambers compositions on side B. AllMusic 5 stars, Penguin Guide 3.5 stars. 'Little B's Poem' is a Hutcherson classic. Session gravity is exceptional — five of the six musicians are already canon leaders. Represents the adventurous mid-60s Blue Note spirit that the canon needs more of.",
    "case_against": "Hutcherson is already represented in the canon by Dialogue (1965) and Stick-Up! (1966); Components falls between them chronologically and stylistically. While excellent, it is not the defining statement of any musician present — Hubbard, Hancock and Carter have stronger canon records. The contested tier reflects strong arguments both ways.",
    "disagreement": "The two sides of the album (Hutcherson hard-bop vs Chambers avant) could be viewed as either a strength (range) or a weakness (split focus). The council sees value in both perspectives.",
    "recommendation": "Include — the session gravity alone makes it a load-bearing record of the Blue Note post-bop era, and the Hutcherson arc benefits from having this bridging album.",
    "confidence": "high"
  }
}